-- The prune's second halves (ADR-0060 §3 and §5, `D28.4`, review finding
-- `DB-07`). Generated by drizzle-kit and unedited but for this comment.
--
-- Each sweep in `prune-sessions.ts` deletes on `expires < cutoff OR
-- consumed/revoked < cutoff`, and Postgres can serve an `OR` with a BitmapOr
-- only when **both** sides have an index. The expiry sides have had theirs
-- since their tables were created; these are the other three, so a 1,000-row
-- batch reads the dead rows rather than scanning the table. Partial, because
-- the column is null on every row that is still live — exactly the rows the
-- sweep never wants — so the index holds only what the predicate asks about
-- and costs nothing on the insert a login or a mint takes.
--
-- **Blocking, deliberately** (ADR-0060 §5, rule 2). Each `CREATE INDEX` is the
-- plain form and holds a `SHARE` lock on its table for the build — reads go
-- on, writes wait — because the migrator runs the pending batch in one
-- transaction and `CONCURRENTLY` cannot run inside one. The table that matters
-- is `sessions`: while its index builds, a login, a refresh and a logout wait,
-- on the **old** API, since `migrate` is the release gate. Milliseconds on the
-- tables this blueprint expects: the build reads the whole table but writes
-- only the rows the predicate admits. A deployment whose `sessions` table is
-- large should apply this release in a maintenance window (`docs/deploy.md`).
CREATE INDEX "account_tokens_consumed_at_idx" ON "account_tokens" USING btree ("consumed_at") WHERE "account_tokens"."consumed_at" is not null;--> statement-breakpoint
CREATE INDEX "impersonation_handoffs_consumed_at_idx" ON "impersonation_handoffs" USING btree ("consumed_at") WHERE "impersonation_handoffs"."consumed_at" is not null;--> statement-breakpoint
CREATE INDEX "sessions_revoked_at_idx" ON "sessions" USING btree ("revoked_at") WHERE "sessions"."revoked_at" is not null;
